  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980753/assets/59c02273515e87fcebb8eb1785dc2cf0c6490feda4fc9644df43b8849921eec5.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\n- $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ exists\n- $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=-1$",
  "worked_solution": "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)$ is defined at $x=-1$ and we have $f(-1)=1.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x) & =2, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x) & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, both left-hand side and right-hand side limits exist but they are not the same. Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)$ does not exist.\n\n- Statement III is false. The function contains a break exactly at $x=-1$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper.",
